Intel I225/I226 NVM Update Port for FreeBSD
============================================

This is an unofficial combination assembled by the FreeBSD port.  Intel did
not publish or support it as an Intel NVM Update Package.

Sources
-------

* The FreeBSD nvmupdate64e 1.43.37.1a executable and Intel license files are
  fetched unmodified from Intel's E610 v1.61 FreeBSD NVM Update Package.
  Although that archive also contains E610 firmware, this port does not install
  any of it.
* The I225 device 15F2 NVM 1.94 images and required EEPROM map are fetched
  unmodified from Intel's I225 v1.00 Linux NVM Update Package.  Intel publishes
  that package inside its complete driver pack rather than as a standalone
  download, so an unchanged copy of the nested archive is hosted in the
  maintainer's FreeBSD distfile directory for use by the ports framework.
* The I225-V device 15F3 NVM 1.89 images and I226-V, I226-LM, and I226-IT NVM
  2.32 images are fetched unmodified from
  BillyCurtis/Intel-I226-V-NVM-Firmware at commit
  5fb02f3128f894fdeb47bea6450af872fb85ff45.  This is an unofficial,
  unsupported GitHub mirror.  Intel's corresponding I226 NVM package is
  access-controlled in RDC.  FreeBSD does not vouch for the mirror's provenance
  or assert that it is authorized by Intel.
* This port installs only the current images named in source_manifest.txt; the
  GitHub snapshot also contains historical images that are not installed.
* The configuration files, README.FreeBSD, source_manifest.txt, and version.txt
  are generated and maintained by the FreeBSD port.  They were not supplied or
  approved by Intel or the GitHub mirror maintainer.

Configuration matching
----------------------

EEPID identifies and verifies the target image.  REPLACES is optional.  Per
Intel's NVMUpdate documentation, a device block without REPLACES wildcard-
matches its exact vendor, device, subvendor, and subdevice IDs.

The Intel-supplied I225 device 15F2 entries and the mirrored I225-V and I226-V
entries in nvmupdate.cfg list known ETrack lineages.  For I225-V and I226-V,
those lineages also distinguish their 1MB and 2MB variants.  The I226-LM and
I226-IT entries omit REPLACES so they can update any NVM version with the
matching four-part PCI ID.  Four single-image configuration files let the
operator select the intended LM/IT flash size explicitly.

Security model
--------------

Intel documents a secure NVM flow for I225/I226 in which secured OROM, PHY, and
firmware modules are signed by Intel and authenticated by the controller.  This
does not establish the provenance of the complete .bin container, its filename,
the GitHub archive, or nvmupdate.cfg as a unit.  The NVMUpdate tool also verifies
images by default; keep that verification enabled and never pass its -sv option.

Scope and safety
----------------

The generated entries intentionally match only the Intel reference subsystem
IDs listed in nvmupdate.cfg.  The Intel package adds I225 device 15F2 subsystem
8086:0001 (retail I225-T1, 2MB) and 8086:0002 (I225-LM, 1MB).  The entries do
not match arbitrary OEM subsystem IDs or I225-IT device 0D9F.

The combined nvmupdate.cfg contains both flash-size candidates for I226-LM and
I226-IT, whose PCI IDs do not reveal flash size.  Do not use the combined file
to update those devices.  Select exactly one of these files with -c after
determining the correct flash size from authoritative platform information:

    nvmupdate-i226-lm-1mb.cfg
    nvmupdate-i226-lm-2mb.cfg
    nvmupdate-i226-it-1mb.cfg
    nvmupdate-i226-it-2mb.cfg

A 2MB backup does not prove that the device has 2MB flash.  Reports in the
OPNsense thread show that NVMUpdate backups can be 2MB for a 1MB device.

Run inventory mode first and save its output:

    nvmupdate-i225-i226 -i -l inventory.log -o inventory.xml

For example, inventory an I226-LM against only the selected 2MB image with:

    nvmupdate-i225-i226 -i -c nvmupdate-i226-lm-2mb.cfg \
        -l inventory.log -o inventory.xml

An update can interrupt networking and requires a complete power cycle.  Make
a backup, use an out-of-band management path, and follow Intel's NVM Update Tool
documentation before choosing to update any device.
